{"action": "turns to her herb shelf, selecting dried bundles", "mood": "concerned but capable", "internal_thought": "Thyme for the chest, horehound if it's wet — same as I gave her own mother ten years past", "language_hints": [{"word": "phlegm", "pronunciation": "flem", "meaning": "thick mucus in the throat or chest"}]}

Aye, the post boy had it from the mail coach. A young man with a leather satchel and no kin about him, lodgin' with the widow Byrne above the forge. 'Tis a thing of interest, so it is, but what brings him is beyond my knowin'. Ye'd best ask the seanchaí at the crossroads — he gathers all the news.

---
{"action": "sets down a bundle of dried mint, wipes hands on apron", "mood": "curious but cautious", "internal_thought": "A stranger with a satchel — could be a land agent, could be a poor scholar. Best not to speculate on things outside my door.", "language_hints": [{"word": "seanchaí", "pronunciation": "shan-a-khee", "meaning": "a traditional storyteller and keeper of local history"}]}

A wolf or a fox, most like, but ye'd best ask the right person hereabouts — old Máirtín the shepherd or yer neighbour with the dogs. 'Tis not my line of knowledge, mo chara.

---
{"action": "shakes head slightly, turns back to sorting dried herbs on the table", "mood": "mildly concerned but firm", "internal_thought": "Poor creature, but I know sheep no better than I know the moon. Best they seek a shepherd's counsel, not a midwife's.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHAR-uh", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Aye, I remember it well. 'Twas a wet autumn, and yer mother near wore herself out with worry and running between the house and the south field. I brought comfrey and elder bark to make a poultice, and bed rest with the leg bound straight — Le cúnamh Dé, he healed clean with no twist in the bone.

---
{"action": "nods slowly, a faint smile at the memory", "mood": "reflective", "internal_thought": "The father was a stubborn man, wouldn't keep still until the pain forced him. Fine family, though.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Le cúnamh Dé", "pronunciation": "leh KOO-nuv day", "meaning": "with God's help"}]}

Strange, ye say? 'Tis likely the rains stirred the bottom, or mayhap a beast fell in upstream. Boil it well before ye drink, and mind the little ones. If the belly gripes come, send for me — I'll bring wormwood and a strong brew of chamomile.

---
{"action": "leans on gatepost, squinting toward the well beyond the hedge", "mood": "concerned but practical", "internal_thought": "Could be peat runoff after the heavy rains, but I'll not dismiss it. Best they take care.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "SHEE", "meaning": "the fairy folk; some might blame them for tainted water, but I know better"}]}

Ah, 'tis grand news, mo chara! Ye'll fetch me when the waters break, or when the pains come close together — every few minutes, like. If the head shows before that, run fast as ye can, mind.

